#lang saffron
;; saffron-lang-plan S5/D5: the Saffron -> Turmeric seam CHECKS rather than
;; erases, and BOTH back ends say the same thing when the check fails.
;;
;; `takes-int` has a real signature and the caller hands it an `any` holding a
;; cstr.  D5 rejected erasure explicitly: passing the payload word through
;; unchecked would turn this type error into a memory-safety bug, because the
;; word is a `const char *` read as an int.  The seam reuses the checked unbox
;; that `(cast x T)` lowers to, so the failure is a panic naming the mismatch.
;;
;; This lives in tests/fixtures/ rather than tests/fixtures/errors/ because the
;; failure is a RUNTIME panic, not a compile diagnostic: the errors/ suite only
;; runs `emit-c` and asserts a non-zero exit from the front end, which this
;; program does not produce -- it compiles fine and then panics.  expected.exit
;; is `nonzero` rather than a number because the two back ends abort
;; differently: the compiled program calls abort() and exits 134, the
;; interpreter exits 1.
;;
;; The MESSAGE is the point.  It used to differ: `__tur_any_type_name` names a
;; primitive on the compiled side, while the interpreter's cast message went
;; through a helper that answers NULL for anything that is not a struct and so
;; printed "a value of a different type" -- the same failure, described two
;; ways, with the useful description on only one of them.  Both now name the
;; type that actually arrived.
(defn takes-int [n : int] : int (+ n 1))
(defn dyn [v] v)
(defn main [] : int
  (println (takes-int (dyn "not an int")))
  0)
